  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Employers Consultative Association of Malawi (ECAM) partnered with the Malawi Congress of Trade Unions (MCTU) to enhance bipartite social dialogue through the adoption a Joint Action Plan and signing a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU). This partnership emerged from the shared recognition by both ECAM and MCTU of the necessity to formulate a collective position and an advocacy strategy pertaining to legislative reforms and employment creation policies. Additionally, the aim was to revitalize the Tripartite Labour Advisory Council (TLAC) which had been long inactive. 

To initiate this endeavour, an Issues Paper was prepared, drawing on insights from focused interviews, literature review, and news articles. This review, conducted in 2022, pinpointed various areas of concern for employers and workers. ECAM subsequently outlined priority policy measures designed to unleash the potential of the private sector as the engine of growth, decent employment creation and the effective social dialogue through TLAC. This proposition was presented at a high-level meeting organized by ECAM and MCTU in Salima in Sept 2022. This gathering brought together 30 representatives of workers and employers. Following an intense dialogue, the social partners developed and adopted a Joint Action Plan capturing the agreed matrix of policy issues of mutual interest. They also adopted a set of recommendations for promoting rights at work, including addressing violence and harassment in the world of work, reinvigorating the national social dialogue institution and mechanisms, and addressing the structural challenges of youth unemployment.   

To popularise the advocacy agenda, the ECAM and MCTU presented the prioritised policy issues, the joint action plan and signed the MoU at a ceremony in Lilongwe in Aug 2023. This event took place in the presence of various stakeholders, including government officials, Workers, and Employers alongside members of the media. The ILO developed the scope of work for the Issues Paper that informed the bipartite dialogue. The ILO further provided technical guidance on the approach to collecting information through 24 interviews with selected government departments, members of MCTU and members of ECAM The ILO reviewed both the Issues Paper before presenting it at the dialogue in Sept 2022 and the Matrix of Policy Issues that came out of the dialogue. The ILO provided coordination support towards organizing the bipartite dialogue which formulated the Matrix of Policy Issues that are mutual to both workers and employers. The ILO provided support by way of quality oversight in the formulation of the final Joint Action Plan.  Importantly, the ILO built the capacity of ECAM by working closely with the nominated staff of ECAM to review and refine the policy issues formulated as well as in finalising the Memorandum of Understanding. Through media interviews and a purposely crafted Press Release, ECAM Secretary General was guided on how to communicate to the wider membership and to government, the importance of the partnership with MCTU and the significance of the key policy issues that were prioritised by the bipartite.</narrative>
   </description>
